Project Goal: Smart Inventory Management using Reinforcement Learning

High-level objective:

The aim is to create an AI agent that can make smart restocking decisions for a retail store.

The agent should balance inventory levels to avoid:

Stockouts (running out of products → lost sales)

Overstock (having too many products → increased storage cost or waste)

Key idea:
Instead of manually deciding how much to restock each day/week, the RL agent learns from experience how the store behaves (sales, demand fluctuations, etc.) and optimizes orders automatically.

How It Works Conceptually

Environment (the store):

Simulates real store operations:

Current stock of each product

Daily customer demand (can be random or based on historical data)

Holding costs (for storing extra stock)

Stockout penalties (lost sales, unhappy customers)

Agent:

Decides how many units to order each day/week.

Observes the state of the store (inventory, sales trends, day of the week, etc.).

Receives a reward based on performance:

profit from sales

− penalty for stockouts

− cost of overstock

Learning process:

The agent explores different ordering strategies.

It learns which actions maximize the cumulative reward over time (i.e., profit and efficiency).

Why RL is useful here

Traditional rule-based methods (e.g., “always order 10 units”) cannot adapt to fluctuations in demand or seasonal changes.

RL can dynamically adjust orders based on experience and patterns.

Over time, the agent learns an optimal policy for ordering that maximizes profit and minimizes waste.
